---
// Figure 1: adding one server under three ways of placing keys, and how much of the cache it empties.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Slider from '../shared/Slider.astro';
import Choice from '../shared/Choice.astro';
import Readout from '../shared/Readout.astro';
import './cluster.css';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="cluster-placement" title="Adding a server: which keys change home" controls="none"
  caption="Illustrative model, not a measurement. 20,000 keys named product:0 to product:19999; the grid shows the first 96. Modulo placement uses CRC16 of the whole key; the ring gives each server 160 points, placed, like the keys, by a well-mixed 32-bit hash (FNV-1a followed by murmur3's finaliser); hash slots use Redis's real CRC16 slots, with the new server taking an equal share of slots from the start of each old server's range. The readouts count all 20,000 keys. Left out: the time and network traffic to copy moved slots, and keys of unequal popularity.">
  <Fragment slot="params">
    <Choice name="scheme" label="Keys are placed by" value="mod"
      options={[{ value: 'mod', label: 'hash mod N' }, { value: 'ring', label: 'a hash ring' }, { value: 'slots', label: 'hash slots' }]} />
    <Slider name="servers" label="Servers before the new one" min={2} max={8} value={4} unit="servers" />
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="stats">
    <Readout name="moved" label="Keys that change server" kind="write" />
    <Readout name="lost" label="Cached keys that now miss" kind="miss" />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="cluster-cell-legend" aria-hidden="true">
    <span><i data-cache-kind="redis" data-fill="tint">B</i>stays on server B</span>
    <span><i data-cache-kind="miss" data-fill="dashed">B→E</i>new home, empty: a miss</span>
    <span><i data-cache-kind="redis" data-fill="solid">B→E</i>moved with its data</span>
  </div>
  <div data-grid></div>
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>Each cell is one cached key, labelled with the server that holds it. Going from 4 servers to 5 with hash mod N sends about 80% of keys to a different server; their new server has never seen them, so 80% of the cache misses at once. A hash ring moves only the keys in the arcs the new server takes over: 18% of keys, close to the fifth that a fifth server takes on average, and those keys miss. With 160 points per server, every server's share stays within a few points of an equal share. With hash slots, the new server takes an equal share of slots, so 20% of keys change server, and because Redis Cluster moves each slot together with its keys, none of them miss.</p>
    <p>In general, adding one server to N moves about N ÷ (N + 1) of keys with hash mod N, and about 1 ÷ (N + 1) with a ring or with hash slots.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { bindParams, setReadouts } from '../shared/player.mjs';
  import { cellGrid } from '../shared/grid.mjs';
  import { addServer, serverName } from './placement-model.mjs';

  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="cluster-placement"]').forEach(root => {
    const grid = cellGrid(root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-grid]')!, {
      label: 'Grid of 96 cached keys, each labelled with its server before and after adding one',
      cell: { width: 46, height: 24, gap: 4 }, minColumns: 6, maxColumns: 12,
    });
    const pct = (v: number) => `${Math.round(v * 100)}%`;
    bindParams(root, (params: any) => {
      const servers = Number(params.servers), scheme = params.scheme;
      const r = addServer(scheme, servers);
      grid.render(r.cells.map(c => {
        const from = serverName(c.before), to = serverName(c.after);
        if (!c.moved) return { kind: 'redis', fill: 'tint', text: from };
        return scheme === 'slots' ? { kind: 'redis', fill: 'solid', text: `${from}→${to}` } : { kind: 'miss', fill: 'dashed', text: `${from}→${to}` };
      }));
      const legend = root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.cluster-cell-legend i');
      const newest = serverName(servers);
      legend[1].textContent = legend[2].textContent = `B→${newest}`;
      setReadouts(root, { moved: pct(r.moved), lost: pct(r.lost) });
    });
  });
</script>
